  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Origin and Escalation
    
    Breach (ice-screamed) began as a seizure and became a war no participant intended. The Qingqe'frostlings, Aurora Stack's post-Incursion governing council, discovered Xueshei's Compass — an artifact whose full capabilities remain disputed — and moved to claim it by force from ☽'shadow Covens' Order at Terrace Momiou, a southern trade terrace built during the Great Thaw. Qingqe'frostlings deployed Gnash and Crumble warriors, and for three cycles held the terrace with the compass in their possession, driving ☽'shadow Covens' Order into deeper terraces where their magic weakened.
    
    The war widened when the Wake-Singers, the orca-aligned blood-cult operating from the eastern reaches, entered on their own terms. The Cell (owl-sworn), a Nightshelf faction, severed supply lines whose importance had not been apparent until they failed. Qingqe'frostlings found themselves fighting on three fronts with an artifact they did not fully understand.
    
    The Rupture-Song — an aurora-channeled ability of considerable destructive scale — was deployed during the conflict and elevated the damage beyond any tactical objective. The Radiant Aurora-harp, the great resonance instrument maintained by Aurora channelers, fell silent; its operators could not sustain the crystal spires under sustained assault. Veil∴numb's interference — insufficiently documented in every source I have consulted — prolonged the destruction past the point where either side retained the capacity to end it deliberately.
    
    ## Conclusion and Aftermath
    
    Breach ended not through surrender but through mutual exhaustion. The compass vanished — dropped, destroyed, or carried deeper into the Berg by someone who did not return. The terraces were shattered, aurora-channels fractured, crystal spires toppled. The ice in those sections still carries the trauma; it hums, reportedly, when the lights cycle wrong.
    
    The physical destruction established legal precedent. The shattered terraces and fractured channels were cited alongside the Mbra∴silentiu's flooded tunnels to justify emergency redistribution of trade licensing and reallocation of route authority under Accord provisions — precedent invoked in factional disputes well after the war itself passed from memory into ice.
    
    Breach is commemorated through the Circle Gathering, the Clear-sky Mandate, the Long-dark Edict, and the Thaw-Kept — four instruments of governance born, in whole or part, from what it cost.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771586841729_0", anchor: "severed supply lines whose importance had not been apparent until they failed", text: "Not apparent to the Aurora factions. The Cell knew precisely what those routes carried and when their failure would bite. A Nightshelf faction does not sever supply lines by accident, and the timing — after the Qingqe'frostlings had committed to three fronts — is surgical. This was an intelligence operation wearing the mask of factional self-interest. The Nightshelf coded dispatches from this period survive in a cipher I can only partially read, but the routing timestamps alone tell the story: the cuts were sequenced, not simultaneous. Someone was watching the war develop and choosing the moment.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771586841729_1", anchor: "dropped, destroyed, or carried deeper into the Berg by someone who did not return", text: "The third option is doing extraordinary work in that sentence, and I let it. The Foundation Depths contain no trace of Xueshei's Compass. I have looked.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771586841729_2", anchor: "entered on their own terms", text: "After the Rupture-Song was deployed. Not before. Blood-ritual practitioners drawn to the site of a massive aurora-channeled detonation — the resonance would have carried to the Corpse Current. They came because something called them. Whether the Rupture-Song's operators understood they were issuing an invitation is a question no survivor has been willing to answer directly.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771586841729_3", anchor: "The shattered terraces and fractured channels were cited alongside", text: "Here. This is where Breach lives now — not in the ice-memory, not in the commemorative edicts, but in the legal footnotes. Every emergency reallocation of trade licensing for the next forty cycles cites this damage as precedent. The terraces were rebuilt. The precedent was not retired. Someone understood early that a devastated terrace is a temporary problem, but an emergency provision is permanent architecture. I suspect it was Qeihong's predecessors, though the Exchange's institutional memory on this point is conveniently threadbare.", type: :commentary, display: :full
